<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice Yaméogo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice Yaméogo was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, leading the territory to independence on 5 August 1960. He established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> under his Voltaic Democratic Union. Austerity measures provoked strikes and mass protests in January 1966, and the army under Sangoulé Lamizana removed him from power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-yameogo.jpg" alt="Maurice Yaméogo" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, Service de Presse et d'Information. France. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Yam%C3%A9ogo,_1960_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to his official biography, Maurice Nawalagmba Yaméogo was born on 31 December 1921 in Koudougou, west of Ouagadougou, with a twin sister, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> peasants. He became interested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a child and was baptised in 1929, taking the name Maurice, and in 1934 he entered the Minor Seminary of Pabré, which produced most of the country's priests and many of its administrators. There he befriended future figures such as Joseph Ki-Zerbo and Joseph Ouédraogo. He left in 1939 without graduating and became a shipping clerk for the French Colonial Administration, later heading the local branch of the French Confederation of Christian Workers.</p><p>Yaméogo was elected general councillor for Koudougou in the territorial assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a> on 15 December 1946, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a>, divided up in 1932, did not exist as a unit. It was restored on 4 September 1947. He joined the Voltaic Union and was elected grand counsellor of French West Africa on 28 July 1948, aged barely twenty-six. After electoral defeats in 1952 and 1956 he was sent back to clerical work. In March 1957 the Voltaic Democratic Movement list he led at Koudougou won all six seats at stake, and he became minister of agriculture in the coalition government of Ouezzin Coulibaly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 20 October 1958 the assembly elected him president of the council. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Republic of Upper Volta</a> was proclaimed on 11 December 1958, and after receiving special powers in January 1959 he dissolved the assembly and won a large majority in the elections of 19 April, with turnout of 47 per cent. He proclaimed independence on 5 August 1960. His regime, described as dictatorial, lost the support of the Catholic church in 1964 and 1965, and his decrees against customary chieftainship were welcomed in the west but angered the east.</p><p>Unions formed an inter-union front against his austerity plans in December 1965 and called a general strike for 3 January 1966; Yaméogo declared a state of emergency on 1 January, but religious authorities and the traditional elite supported the movement. He left the presidency on 3 January 1966, was placed under house arrest on 6 January, and was sentenced in 1969 to five years of forced labour and loss of civil rights. Sangoulé Lamizana pardoned him, and he was freed on 5 August 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1977 he founded the National Union for the Defense of Democracy, based on nostalgia for the first republic, which became the second-largest party in the legislative elections that year. He was brought to be shot after Thomas Sankara's revolution on 9 November 1983 but survived through the intervention of Blaise Compaoré, and was released in 1984. He died on 15 September 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice Yaméogo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Maurice Yaméogo</b> was the first president and banned all parties but his own.</blockquote><a href="/state/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a></li><li><blockquote>Upper Volta became an autonomous republic in 1958 and independent on 5 August 1960 under <b>Maurice Yameogo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-upper-volta">French Upper Volta</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Koudougou, in what is now <a href="/countries/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.25,-2.37&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Koudougou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4485 1707 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maurice Yaméogo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4935 1977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4935 1977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.25,-2.37&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
